// The seam table: every seam the runtime realises, one domain per file.
//
// A seam is a function of the client with a Before and an After, both realised by both builds:
// on x86 a detour at the function's resolved address calls Before, the original, then After; on
// x64 the recompiled client's own source calls Before where the function starts and After where
// it returns. The body of each is written once, in src/seams/<Domain>.cpp.
//
// There are two row shapes, and a seam is one or the other:
//
//   WXL_SEAM(domain, name, symbol, ret, conv, params)       an observing seam: Before, After
//   WXL_SEAM_GATE(domain, name, symbol, ret, conv, params)  a gated seam: Gate
//
// An observing seam watches. It is told the call started and that it finished, and changes
// neither. A gated seam stands in the doorway: its single entry point, Gate, is handed every
// argument by address and answers whether it has taken the call -- taken means the function
// does not run at all and the seam has said what it answers instead; not taken means the
// function runs, with whatever the seam left at those addresses, which is also how an argument
// is rewritten.
//
// A gated seam has no After, deliberately. On x64 the call sites are the client's own source,
// and an After would have to be written at every return of the function rather than once at
// its head; a seam that wants to watch a function watches it with the observing shape. So the
// choice between the two is: does a promise of the contract need the call stopped or an
// argument changed? If not, observe.
//
// A row whose parameter is a client type the boundary cannot name crosses as void*, and the
// x64 call site then passes the address of a local copy of it: such an argument can be read
// and not usefully rewritten. A row that means to be rewritten declares a type both sides
// spell the same, as a path does.
//
// Include this with both macros defined. This file holds nothing but the domain tables, in the
// order below; the columns are described in world.def.
